<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Free City of Krakow</h1><p class="st-meta">1815 CE – 1846 CE · capital Krakow</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small city republic around Krakow under the joint protection of Austria, Prussia and Russia, the last free remnant of Poland.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Unable to agree who should have <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Krakow</a>, the powers at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1815 made the city and its surrounding district a free, independent and strictly neutral city under the protection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Krakow</a> had a senate, an assembly and its own constitution, though the three protecting powers supervised it through their residents. It was the only place where Polish institutions functioned freely, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-university">Jagiellonian University</a> was a centre of Polish culture. Its free-trade status helped commerce, and it became a refuge for Polish patriots and conspirators.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In February 1846 a national uprising broke out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Krakow</a> and quickly collapsed. In November 1846 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> annexed the city with the agreement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, joining it to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-galicia-and-lodomeria">Kingdom of Galicia and Lodomeria</a> as the Grand <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-krakow">Duchy of Krakow</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-city-of-krakow.jpg" alt="Free City of Krakow" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Krak%C3%B3w(2-3).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1815 CE – 1846 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Krakow</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Stanislaw Wodzicki</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Polish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Poland</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Krakow, Free, Independent and Strictly Neutral City of Krakow</dd></dl></aside></div>
